1
00:00:00,552 --> 00:00:01,141
Sir.

2
00:00:01,181 --> 00:00:03,558
A new field report from planet Earth.

3
00:00:03,615 --> 00:00:04,368
Today's subject:

4
00:00:04,408 --> 00:00:05,428
cryptocurrency.

5
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:07,393
Money the humans invented without a bank.

6
00:00:07,433 --> 00:00:08,422
And without paper.

7
00:00:08,462 --> 00:00:09,594
I will tell you what it is, where

8
00:00:09,634 --> 00:00:10,931
it came from, and who made it.

9
00:00:10,971 --> 00:00:12,483
And how one of them paid for two

10
00:00:12,523 --> 00:00:14,636
pizzas with what is now worth hundreds of

11
00:00:14,676 --> 00:00:15,568
millions of dollars.

12
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:16,525
The year 2010.

13
00:00:16,565 --> 00:00:17,901
Ten thousand bitcoin...

14
00:00:17,941 --> 00:00:19,030
for two pizzas.

15
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:20,298
At the prices of recent years, that comes

16
00:00:20,338 --> 00:00:21,984
to hundreds of millions of dollars.

17
00:00:22,024 --> 00:00:23,515
The most expensive dinner on the planet.

18
00:00:23,555 --> 00:00:24,448
And not even deep dish.

19
00:00:24,488 --> 00:00:26,310
But for Command to understand any of this,

20
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:27,706
I have to start with the first thing

21
00:00:27,746 --> 00:00:28,569
I studied here:

22
00:00:28,609 --> 00:00:29,537
money itself.

23
00:00:29,577 --> 00:00:30,410
I checked their history.

24
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:32,028
Long ago they swapped things.

25
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:33,212
A fish for a loaf of bread.

26
00:00:33,252 --> 00:00:33,829
The problem:

27
00:00:33,869 --> 00:00:35,510
if the baker wants no fish today, you

28
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:36,051
go hungry.

29
00:00:36,091 --> 00:00:36,815
Holding a fish.

30
00:00:36,855 --> 00:00:38,834
So they tried other things everybody would take.

31
00:00:38,874 --> 00:00:39,308
Salt.

32
00:00:39,348 --> 00:00:39,860
Shells.

33
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,288
And on one island, stones as big as

34
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:41,863
a car.

35
00:00:41,903 --> 00:00:43,024
Imagine asking for change.

36
00:00:43,064 --> 00:00:44,323
They settled on gold:

37
00:00:44,363 --> 00:00:46,888
rare, never rots, hard to fake.

38
00:00:47,373 --> 00:00:48,307
But gold is heavy.

39
00:00:48,347 --> 00:00:50,002
So they left it with someone trusted and

40
00:00:50,042 --> 00:00:50,696
took a receipt.

41
00:00:50,736 --> 00:00:52,436
The receipt started changing hands:

42
00:00:52,476 --> 00:00:53,157
paper money.

43
00:00:53,197 --> 00:00:54,712
Then the trusted man discovered he could lend

44
00:00:54,752 --> 00:00:56,018
your gold to someone else while you were

45
00:00:56,058 --> 00:00:56,539
not looking.

46
00:00:56,579 --> 00:00:57,690
And that, sir, is how the bank was

47
00:00:57,730 --> 00:00:58,034
born.

48
00:00:58,074 --> 00:01:00,290
In 1971 the dollar was cut loose from

49
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:00,771
gold.

50
00:01:00,811 --> 00:01:01,980
The paper stood on its own.

51
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,591
And whoever runs the printing press can print

52
00:01:03,631 --> 00:01:04,630
as much as they like.

53
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:06,291
In Zimbabwe, they printed a single note for

54
00:01:06,331 --> 00:01:07,787
one hundred trillion dollars.

55
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:09,247
It would not buy a bus ticket.

56
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:11,399
So money's value comes from one thing:

57
00:01:11,439 --> 00:01:12,465
everyone believes in it.

58
00:01:12,505 --> 00:01:13,472
A group agreement.

59
00:01:13,512 --> 00:01:14,861
Like a line at the coffee shop.

60
00:01:14,901 --> 00:01:16,613
It works until somebody cuts.

61
00:01:16,653 --> 00:01:18,125
And today most of their money is not

62
00:01:18,165 --> 00:01:18,882
even paper.

63
00:01:18,922 --> 00:01:20,051
It is numbers on a screen.

64
00:01:20,091 --> 00:01:21,660
Most money on this planet has no physical

65
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:22,069
form.

66
00:01:22,109 --> 00:01:22,990
Entries in tables.

67
00:01:23,030 --> 00:01:24,248
And they are perfectly comfortable with that.

68
00:01:24,288 --> 00:01:25,692
I also recorded that their money has three

69
00:01:25,732 --> 00:01:26,260
jobs.

70
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:26,903
You pay with it.

71
00:01:26,943 --> 00:01:27,970
You price things with it.

72
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:29,169
And you save it for tomorrow.

73
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:30,483
Keep those three in the file, sir.

74
00:01:30,523 --> 00:01:31,342
We will need them later.

75
00:01:31,382 --> 00:01:33,388
I traced those numbers to where they actually

76
00:01:33,428 --> 00:01:33,823
live:

77
00:01:33,863 --> 00:01:34,445
in a ledger.

78
00:01:34,485 --> 00:01:36,331
Kept by an entity called the bank.

79
00:01:36,371 --> 00:01:39,753
When Alice pays Bob thirty dollars, nothing moves.

80
00:01:39,793 --> 00:01:42,014
The bank erases a number here, writes one

81
00:01:42,054 --> 00:01:42,470
there.

82
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:43,321
Your salary:

83
00:01:43,361 --> 00:01:43,898
a number.

84
00:01:43,938 --> 00:01:44,524
Your rent:

85
00:01:44,564 --> 00:01:45,140
a number.

86
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,246
You have never actually seen this money.

87
00:01:47,286 --> 00:01:48,991
Every bank keeps its own ledger.

88
00:01:49,031 --> 00:01:50,674
So every night they check each other's.

89
00:01:50,714 --> 00:01:52,269
Because none of them trusts the others.

90
00:01:52,309 --> 00:01:53,824
So a bank is not a vault full

91
00:01:53,864 --> 00:01:54,303
of money.

92
00:01:54,343 --> 00:01:55,551
A bank is a ledger.

93
00:01:55,591 --> 00:01:57,608
And whoever holds the ledger holds everything.

94
00:01:57,648 --> 00:01:59,994
I followed one transfer from start to finish.

95
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:02,018
Every cent that moves between two humans has

96
00:02:02,058 --> 00:02:03,638
to pass through a third party.

97
00:02:03,678 --> 00:02:04,518
Alice sends.

98
00:02:04,558 --> 00:02:05,718
The bank checks.

99
00:02:05,758 --> 00:02:06,976
Then Bob receives it.

100
00:02:07,016 --> 00:02:07,891
Minus the fee.

101
00:02:07,931 --> 00:02:10,198
The ledger holder can charge a fee, delay

102
00:02:10,238 --> 00:02:12,881
you, freeze your account, or go bankrupt with

103
00:02:12,921 --> 00:02:13,707
your money.

104
00:02:13,747 --> 00:02:14,980
This is not a theory.

105
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:17,147
On the island of Cyprus, in 2013, people

106
00:02:17,187 --> 00:02:18,798
woke up to find the banks closed.

107
00:02:18,838 --> 00:02:20,525
Anyone with over a hundred thousand euros in

108
00:02:20,565 --> 00:02:22,863
the biggest bank lost almost half the excess.

109
00:02:22,903 --> 00:02:23,900
To rescue the bank.

110
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:26,327
In Lebanon, since 2019, banks have locked up

111
00:02:26,367 --> 00:02:27,212
people's dollars.

112
00:02:27,252 --> 00:02:28,949
Years later, they are still asking.

113
00:02:28,989 --> 00:02:31,465
Their messages cross the planet in a second.

114
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:33,834
A bank transfer can take three days.

115
00:02:33,874 --> 00:02:35,759
Both are just numbers, sir.

116
00:02:36,194 --> 00:02:38,177
And over a billion adults here have no

117
00:02:38,217 --> 00:02:38,935
bank account.

118
00:02:38,975 --> 00:02:40,096
No line in the ledger.

119
00:02:40,136 --> 00:02:41,857
As far as the system is concerned, they

120
00:02:41,897 --> 00:02:42,787
do not exist.

121
00:02:42,827 --> 00:02:44,974
So I asked the obvious question:

122
00:02:45,014 --> 00:02:46,828
why not digital money without a bank?

123
00:02:46,868 --> 00:02:47,495
A file.

124
00:02:47,535 --> 00:02:48,521
You send it, done.

125
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:50,191
The problem is that anything digital can be

126
00:02:50,231 --> 00:02:50,851
copied.

127
00:02:50,891 --> 00:02:52,673
A photo, a song, a movie.

128
00:02:52,713 --> 00:02:53,714
Copy, paste.

129
00:02:53,754 --> 00:02:54,120
Free.

130
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,798
If money were a file, Alice could send

131
00:02:55,838 --> 00:02:57,940
the same hundred dollars to Bob, and to

132
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:58,440
Carol.

133
00:02:58,480 --> 00:02:59,040
At once.

134
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,696
That is called the double-spending problem.

135
00:03:00,736 --> 00:03:01,917
And that is why they needed someone in

136
00:03:01,957 --> 00:03:04,042
the middle, keeping track of who paid what.

137
00:03:04,082 --> 00:03:05,595
In the nineties, a group of programmers who

138
00:03:05,635 --> 00:03:08,703
called themselves the Cypherpunks dreamed of private digital

139
00:03:08,743 --> 00:03:09,049
money.

140
00:03:09,089 --> 00:03:09,961
Nobody watching it.

141
00:03:10,001 --> 00:03:11,113
Nobody able to stop it.

142
00:03:11,153 --> 00:03:12,667
Humans had been trying since the eighties.

143
00:03:12,707 --> 00:03:14,709
A scientist named David Chaum started a digital

144
00:03:14,749 --> 00:03:15,443
cash company.

145
00:03:15,483 --> 00:03:17,144
It went bankrupt in 1998.

146
00:03:17,184 --> 00:03:18,954
The digital money ran out of money.

147
00:03:18,994 --> 00:03:21,196
And then came the thing that changed everything:

148
00:03:21,236 --> 00:03:22,034
2008.

149
00:03:22,074 --> 00:03:24,636
Big American banks had bet people's money on

150
00:03:24,676 --> 00:03:25,834
home loans...

151
00:03:25,883 --> 00:03:26,648
and lost.

152
00:03:26,688 --> 00:03:28,506
A bank called Lehman Brothers, more than a

153
00:03:28,546 --> 00:03:30,942
hundred and fifty years old, went under in

154
00:03:30,982 --> 00:03:31,887
a single day.

155
00:03:31,927 --> 00:03:34,575
The American government set aside seven hundred billion

156
00:03:34,615 --> 00:03:36,283
dollars to rescue the banks.

157
00:03:36,323 --> 00:03:37,214
With whose money?

158
00:03:37,254 --> 00:03:38,298
The same people's.

159
00:03:38,338 --> 00:03:40,272
Millions lost their homes and jobs.

160
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:41,372
And the rescued banks?

161
00:03:41,412 --> 00:03:43,649
Nine of them paid over thirty billion dollars

162
00:03:43,689 --> 00:03:45,384
in bonuses that same year.

163
00:03:45,424 --> 00:03:47,523
So the ledger keeper gambled, and the customer

164
00:03:47,563 --> 00:03:47,906
paid.

165
00:03:47,946 --> 00:03:48,895
Heads, the bank wins.

166
00:03:48,935 --> 00:03:49,757
Tails, you pay.

167
00:03:49,797 --> 00:03:50,363
Brilliant, sir.

168
00:03:50,403 --> 00:03:51,313
I recommend we adopt it.

169
00:03:51,353 --> 00:03:53,262
Right in the middle of that crisis, a

170
00:03:53,302 --> 00:03:56,139
nine-page paper appeared, sent to a mailing list

171
00:03:56,179 --> 00:03:57,788
of people interested in cryptography.

172
00:03:57,828 --> 00:03:58,289
The date:

173
00:03:58,329 --> 00:04:00,301
October 31st, 2008.

174
00:04:00,341 --> 00:04:00,835
Halloween.

175
00:04:00,875 --> 00:04:02,997
A masked stranger handing out something free.

176
00:04:03,037 --> 00:04:04,471
Their children are warned about this.

177
00:04:04,511 --> 00:04:04,989
The title:

178
00:04:05,029 --> 00:04:07,386
Bitcoin, a peer-to-peer electronic cash system.

179
00:04:07,426 --> 00:04:08,190
One promise:

180
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:10,868
send money to anyone, directly, asking nobody's permission.

181
00:04:10,908 --> 00:04:11,476
The author:

182
00:04:11,516 --> 00:04:12,804
Satoshi Nakamoto.

183
00:04:12,844 --> 00:04:14,850
To this day, nobody knows who that is.

184
00:04:14,890 --> 00:04:16,617
A man, a woman, or a group.

185
00:04:16,657 --> 00:04:18,015
And before anyone asks, sir:

186
00:04:18,055 --> 00:04:19,201
no, it was not me.

187
00:04:19,241 --> 00:04:19,938
I arrived later.

188
00:04:19,978 --> 00:04:21,898
The idea was simple and bold:

189
00:04:21,938 --> 00:04:23,654
take the ledger away from the bank.

190
00:04:23,694 --> 00:04:25,753
Every computer on the network gets a full

191
00:04:25,793 --> 00:04:26,997
copy of the ledger.

192
00:04:27,037 --> 00:04:29,234
Thousands of computers, all around the planet.

193
00:04:29,274 --> 00:04:31,574
When Alice pays Bob, the news reaches them

194
00:04:31,614 --> 00:04:34,141
all, and every computer writes the same line.

195
00:04:34,181 --> 00:04:35,738
And if someone tries to cheat by editing

196
00:04:35,778 --> 00:04:37,336
their own copy, everyone else says:

197
00:04:37,376 --> 00:04:37,708
no.

198
00:04:37,748 --> 00:04:38,956
That is not what we have.

199
00:04:38,996 --> 00:04:40,367
The ledger is also public.

200
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:41,896
Anyone can read it all, from the first

201
00:04:41,936 --> 00:04:42,431
line.

202
00:04:42,471 --> 00:04:43,859
But the names are not names.

203
00:04:43,899 --> 00:04:45,906
They are long strings of letters and numbers.

204
00:04:45,946 --> 00:04:47,911
Everyone sees everything, and nobody knows who is

205
00:04:47,951 --> 00:04:48,243
who.

206
00:04:48,283 --> 00:04:49,733
A masked ball, with receipts.

207
00:04:49,773 --> 00:04:50,367
No manager.

208
00:04:50,407 --> 00:04:51,156
No head office.

209
00:04:51,196 --> 00:04:52,702
And no customer service line keeping you on

210
00:04:52,742 --> 00:04:54,498
hold for forty minutes with smooth jazz.

211
00:04:54,538 --> 00:04:56,873
Which also means nobody can freeze your account,

212
00:04:56,913 --> 00:04:59,145
or print extra bitcoin to cover a deficit.

213
00:04:59,185 --> 00:05:00,946
The rules are written in code, and the

214
00:05:00,986 --> 00:05:02,461
code is out in the open.

215
00:05:02,501 --> 00:05:04,714
This shared ledger is called the blockchain.

216
00:05:04,754 --> 00:05:06,579
And how thousands of strangers agree on one

217
00:05:06,619 --> 00:05:07,916
copy without trusting each other?

218
00:05:07,956 --> 00:05:09,511
That is the subject of the next report.

219
00:05:09,551 --> 00:05:11,227
January 3rd, 2009.

220
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:13,083
Satoshi switched the network on, and wrote the

221
00:05:13,123 --> 00:05:14,212
first page of the ledger:

222
00:05:14,252 --> 00:05:15,287
block number zero.

223
00:05:15,327 --> 00:05:17,537
And inside that block, Satoshi left a message.

224
00:05:17,577 --> 00:05:19,393
The front-page headline of The Times of London

225
00:05:19,433 --> 00:05:19,904
that day:

226
00:05:19,944 --> 00:05:22,297
Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks.

227
00:05:22,337 --> 00:05:24,039
So the very first line of money without

228
00:05:24,079 --> 00:05:24,503
banks...

229
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:25,607
is a dig at the banks.

230
00:05:25,647 --> 00:05:27,109
This creature had a sense of humor, sir.

231
00:05:27,149 --> 00:05:29,286
That block also created fifty brand new bitcoin.

232
00:05:29,326 --> 00:05:29,975
Out of thin air.

233
00:05:30,015 --> 00:05:31,659
But under rules written down from day one,

234
00:05:31,699 --> 00:05:33,139
which no single person can change.

235
00:05:33,179 --> 00:05:34,387
Quite different from the printing they are used

236
00:05:34,427 --> 00:05:34,623
to.

237
00:05:34,663 --> 00:05:36,150
Nine days later came the first transfer:

238
00:05:36,190 --> 00:05:38,118
ten bitcoin, to a programmer named Hal Finney.

239
00:05:38,158 --> 00:05:39,559
The first two people on the network.

240
00:05:39,599 --> 00:05:40,616
One of them anonymous.

241
00:05:40,656 --> 00:05:41,917
At first, nobody cared.

242
00:05:41,957 --> 00:05:43,726
Bitcoin was practically given away.

243
00:05:43,766 --> 00:05:45,350
The first exchange rate on record:

244
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:46,900
more than a thousand bitcoin...

245
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:47,795
for one dollar.

246
00:05:47,835 --> 00:05:49,190
Until May 2010.

247
00:05:49,230 --> 00:05:51,646
A programmer named Laszlo posted online:

248
00:05:51,686 --> 00:05:53,978
ten thousand bitcoin to whoever gets me two

249
00:05:54,018 --> 00:05:54,623
pizzas.

250
00:05:54,663 --> 00:05:55,942
And on May 22nd...

251
00:05:55,982 --> 00:05:56,757
somebody did.

252
00:05:56,797 --> 00:05:58,644
It was the first time bitcoin bought something

253
00:05:58,684 --> 00:05:59,171
real.

254
00:05:59,211 --> 00:06:01,075
Laszlo later said he has no regrets.

255
00:06:01,115 --> 00:06:01,939
The pizza was good.

256
00:06:01,979 --> 00:06:03,722
And the humans still celebrate that day every

257
00:06:03,762 --> 00:06:04,089
year.

258
00:06:04,129 --> 00:06:05,645
They call it Bitcoin Pizza Day.

259
00:06:05,685 --> 00:06:07,008
They hold a party for the worst deal

260
00:06:07,048 --> 00:06:07,803
in their history.

261
00:06:07,843 --> 00:06:09,119
I am starting to like this planet.

262
00:06:09,159 --> 00:06:11,032
Less than a year later, one bitcoin reached

263
00:06:11,072 --> 00:06:11,782
one dollar.

264
00:06:11,822 --> 00:06:12,636
A whole dollar.

265
00:06:12,676 --> 00:06:14,184
And the humans thought that was the top.

266
00:06:14,224 --> 00:06:15,531
As for Satoshi:

267
00:06:15,571 --> 00:06:18,230
in April 2011 came the last known message.

268
00:06:18,270 --> 00:06:19,821
I've moved on to other things.

269
00:06:19,861 --> 00:06:20,629
Then, gone.

270
00:06:20,669 --> 00:06:23,173
First, Satoshi handed the code to other programmers.

271
00:06:23,213 --> 00:06:24,047
No farewell.

272
00:06:24,087 --> 00:06:25,338
No press conference.

273
00:06:25,378 --> 00:06:26,255
Not even a photo.

274
00:06:26,295 --> 00:06:28,799
And left behind about a million bitcoin, by

275
00:06:28,839 --> 00:06:30,040
researchers' estimates.

276
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,435
As far as the humans can tell, those

277
00:06:31,475 --> 00:06:32,841
coins have never moved.

278
00:06:32,881 --> 00:06:34,438
Invented a new money, became one of the

279
00:06:34,478 --> 00:06:36,426
richest beings on the planet, and never spent

280
00:06:36,466 --> 00:06:37,029
a cent.

281
00:06:37,069 --> 00:06:38,343
Not human behavior, sir.

282
00:06:38,383 --> 00:06:39,727
I request an investigation.

283
00:06:39,767 --> 00:06:41,239
Satoshi may be one of ours.

